;;; A written `(not (eq …))` is stored as a negation, not read as `neq` —
;;; **W51**, regression `not-eq-fact` (`corpus/regressions.toml`)
;;; (Q-M1e2.26).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact `(p a)` and the written negation `(not (eq a a))`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `eq`'s facts are computed, and so is the negation of one: `(not (eq x y))`
;;; is `(neq x y)` (Q-M1f.31). A written one is checked as a conclusion with no
;;; premise (Q-M1f.33): `(neq a a)` is false, so root is a contradiction,
;;; `(false)` written where it stands. R is empty.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `dc4c0bdc` (`ein solve FILE` and `ein oracle FILE`, release and debug
;;; alike), the negation was stored: root held `(not (eq a a))` (`ein saturate
;;; FILE --dump`), no contradiction paired it, H was empty and R was {∅}, exit 0.
;;; The engine reads it as `(neq a a)`: R is empty.
;;;

(relation p Node :why "{?1} is a node")
(p a)
(not (eq a a))
